Let the frequency of the sound is $f$.
In first case, $f=100 \pm 5$
=105 \mathrm{~Hz} \text { or } 95 \mathrm{~Hz}

The frequency of second harmonic $=2 f$
According to second case.

\begin{aligned}
2 f &=200 \pm 10 \\
&=210 \mathrm{~Hz} \text { or } 190 \mathrm{~Hz}
\end{aligned}

If 5 beats are produced with frequency $105 \mathrm{~Hz}$, then frequency of second harmonic will be $210 \mathrm{~Hz}$.
i.e.
$2 f=210\Rightarrow \quad f=105 \mathrm{~Hz}=105 \mathrm{~s}^{-1}$

The velocity of sound in a gas at a fixed temperature is given by

v=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M}}

where, $\gamma=\left(\frac{C_{p}}{C_{V}}\right)$ is specific heat ratio and $M$ is the molecular mass of the gas.
Let, velocity of sound in hydrogen, $v_{1}=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M_{1}}}$
Velocity of sound in oxygen, $v_{2}=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M_{2}}}$
Let, $M_{1}$ and $M_{2}$ be the molecular mass of hydrogen and oxygen respectively $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$ be the moles of hydrogen and oxygen, respectively.
Molecular mass of the mixture,

M_{\text {mix }}=\frac{n_{1} M_{1}+n_{2} M_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}

According to the question, $n_{1}=n_{2}$ at given NTP.
(given)

\rightarrow \quad \ddot{u}_{\text {aix }}=\sqrt{\frac{2 \gamma R T}{17 M_{1}}}

$\therefore$ The ratio of velocity of sound in mixture to that of hydrogen

\begin{aligned}
\frac{v_{1}}{v_{\operatorname{mix}}} &=\frac{\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M_{1}}}}{\sqrt{\frac{\gamma 2 R T}{17 M_{1}}}} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \frac{v_{\text {mix }}}{v_{1}} &=\sqrt{\frac{2}{17}}
\end{aligned}

Given, time interval between echoes $=1 \mathrm{~s}$
Speed of sound in air, $v=340 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$
Let the distance between man and cliff be $x$.
Time taken by sound to hit cliff and return $=\frac{2 x}{v}$
\begin{aligned}
1 &=\frac{2 x}{v} \\
\Rightarrow \quad x &=\frac{340}{2} \mathrm{~m}=170 \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}

Thus, distance between cliffs

=170 \times 2=340 \mathrm{~m}

Given, speed of sound in air, $c=300 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$
Difference between apparent frequency during approach and apparent frequency duifing recession $=2 \%$
Thus, frequency during approach - frequency during recession $=\frac{2}{100} \times f_{0}$
\Rightarrow \quad f_{0}\left(\frac{c+v}{c}\right)-f_{0}\left(\frac{c-v}{c}\right)=\frac{2}{100} f_{0}

where, $c$ is speed of sound in air and $v$ is the velocity of source.

\begin{array}{llrl}
\Rightarrow & & \frac{2 v}{300} & =\frac{2}{100} \\
\Rightarrow & v & =3 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}
\end{array}

Equalion of simple harmonic wave is given as

y=6 \sin 2 \pi(2 t-0.1 x)

Path difference, $\Delta x=2 \mathrm{~mm}$
Comparing with standard equation of wave,

\begin{aligned}
y &=A \sin (\omega t-k x) \\
&=A \sin 2 \pi\left(v t-\frac{k}{2 \pi} \cdot x\right) \\
\frac{k}{2 \pi} &=0.1 \\
k &=0.2 \pi
\end{aligned}

Wavelength, $\lambda=\frac{2 \pi}{k}=\frac{2 \pi}{0.2 \pi}=10 \mathrm{~mm}\therefore$ We know that, phase difference,

\begin{aligned}
\phi &=\frac{2 \pi}{\lambda} \times \text { path difference }(\Delta x) \\
&=\frac{2 \pi}{10} \times 2=\frac{2 \pi}{5}=\frac{2 \times 180}{5}=72^{\circ}
\end{aligned}

Given, tension is the wire, $T=100 \mathrm{~N}$
Mass of steel wire, $m=6 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{~kg}$
Iength, $l=90 \mathrm{~cm}=0.9 \mathrm{~m}$
Mass per unit length of steel wire,

\begin{aligned}
\mu &=\frac{m}{l} \\
&=\frac{6 \times 10^{-3}}{0.9} \\
&=\frac{2}{3} \times 10^{-2} \mathrm{~kg}-\mathrm{m}^{-1}
\end{aligned}

Speed of transverse wave,

\begin{aligned}
v &=\sqrt{\frac{T}{\mu}}=\sqrt{\frac{100}{\left(\begin{array}{c}
2 \\
3
\end{array}\right) \times 10^{-2}}} \\
&=\frac{300}{\sqrt{6}}=50 \sqrt{6} \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}
\end{aligned}

Given, wavelength of sodium line,

\lambda=589.0 \mathrm{~nm}=5.89 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~m}

Observed wavelength,

\lambda^{\prime}=589.6 \mathrm{~nm}=5.896 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~m}

$\therefore$ Change in wavelength,

\begin{aligned}
\Delta \lambda &=\lambda^{\prime}-\lambda \\
&=5.896 \times 10^{-7}-5.89 \times 10^{-7} \\
&=0.006 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~m}=6 \times 10^{-10} \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}

According to Doppler's shift equation,
Speed of galaxy is given as

\begin{aligned}
v &=\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} \times c \\
&=\frac{6 \times 10^{-10}}{5.89 \times 10^{-7}} \times 3 \times 10^{8} \\
&=3.06 \times 10^{5} \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s} \\
&=306 \times 10^{3} \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}=306 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{s}
\end{aligned}

Given, frequency, $f=4.2 \mathrm{MHz}=4.2 \times 10^{6} \mathrm{~Hz}$ Velocity of sound, $v=1.7 \mathrm{kms}^{-1}=1.7 \times 10^{3} \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$ The wavelength of sound in the tissue is given as

\begin{aligned}
\lambda &=\frac{v}{f} \quad(v=f \lambda) \\
&=\frac{1.7 \times 10^{3}}{4.2 \times 10^{6}}=4 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}

Given, velocity of sound, $v=330 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}$
Let frequency of horn be $f$ and speed of the car be $v_{c}$. The frequency of the horn of the car heard by the policeman before it crosses him is given as

f^{\prime}=f\left(\frac{v}{v-v_{c}}\right) \quad \text{...(i)}

and after it crosses him is given as

f^{\prime \prime}=f\left(\frac{v}{v+v_{c}}\right) \quad \text{...(ii)}

From Eqs. (i) and (ii), we get

\begin{aligned}
\frac{f^{\prime \prime}}{f^{\prime}} &=\frac{v-v_{c}}{v+v_{c}}=\frac{330-v_{c}}{330+v_{c}} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \frac{f^{\prime \prime}}{f^{\prime}} &=\frac{330-v_{c}}{330+v_{c}} \quad \text{...(iii)}
\end{aligned}

Since, $f^{\prime \prime}=f^{\prime}-15 \%$ of $f^{\prime}=0.85 f^{\prime}\therefore$ From Eq. (iii), we get

\frac{0.85 f^{\prime}}{f^{\prime}}=\frac{330-v_{c}}{330+v_{c}}

\Rightarrow \quad 0.85=\frac{330-v_{c}}{330+v_{c}} \Rightarrow v_{c}=26.7 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}

Velocity of sound in a gas is given by

v=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M}}

where, $\gamma=$ specific heat ratio
and $M=$ molecular mass of gas.
Fror hydrogen, $v_{H}=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M_{1}}}$
For mixture of gases, molecular mass,
Here,

M_{\mathrm{mix}}=\frac{n_{1} M_{1}+n_{2} M_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}

n_{1}=n_{2}

(for equal volumes)
and $\quad M_{2}=16 M_{1} \quad$ (given)

M_{\text {mix }}=\frac{M_{1}+16 M_{1}}{2}=\frac{17 M_{1}}{2}

$\therefore$ Velocity of sound, $v_{\operatorname{mix}}=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M_{\operatorname{mix}}}}$
Dividing Eq. (ii) by Eq. (i) we get

\begin{aligned}
\frac{v_{\operatorname{mix}}}{v_{H}} &=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M_{\operatorname{mix}}}} \times \sqrt{\frac{M_{1}}{\gamma R T}} \\
&=\sqrt{\frac{M_{1}}{M_{\text {mix }}}}=\sqrt{\frac{2}{17}}
\end{aligned}

The resultant amplitude after super position of two wave differing in phase by $\phi$ is given by

\begin{array}{ll} 
& A_{R}^{2}=A_{1}^{2}+A_{2}^{2}+2 A_{1} A_{2} \cos \phi \\
\Rightarrow & A_{1}=A_{2}=A_{R}=A \\
\Rightarrow & A^{2}=2 A^{2}+2 A^{2} \cos \phi \\
\Rightarrow & \cos \phi=-\frac{1}{2} \\
\Rightarrow & \phi=\pi-\frac{\pi}{3}=\frac{2 \pi}{3}
\end{array}

Speed of sound in a gas, $v=\sqrt{\frac{R T}{M}}$
Both, hydrogen and oxygen gas are diatomic. At same temperature,

\begin{aligned}
& v \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{M}} \\
\therefore \quad & \frac{v_{\mathrm{H}}}{v_{\mathrm{O}}}=\sqrt{\frac{M_{\mathrm{O}}}{M_{\mathrm{H}}}}=\sqrt{\frac{32}{2}}=\sqrt{16}=4 \text { or } 4: 1
\end{aligned}

Given, $\frac{I_{A}}{I_{B}}=100$
Using magnitude equation,

\begin{aligned}
m_{B}-m_{A} &=-2.5 \log \left(\frac{I_{B}}{I_{A}}\right) \\
&=-2.5 \log \left(\frac{1}{100}\right)=-2.5 \times-2=5
\end{aligned}
